# CLAUDE.md

Instructions for Claude Code. Apply these preferences in every session, across all projects.

## Git / commits

- Never commit or push code without explicit user approval, even for small or already-discussed changes.
- Write commit messages and PR titles in [Conventional Commits 1.0.0](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/) format: `<type>[optional scope][!]: <description>`.
- Never sign commits, PR bodies, or issue text as Claude or Cursor: no `Co-Authored-By`/`Co-authored-by` trailers (`noreply@anthropic.com`, `cursoragent@cursor.com`), no `Claude-Session:` line, no "Generated with Claude Code" footer, no session URL.

## Code comments

- Comments describe only the final code as it stands. Never narrate history ("no longer", "used to", "instead of", "was removed", "still", "kept"). When something changes, rewrite the comment for the new state.

## Markdown formatting

- Never hard-wrap inside a paragraph. Write each paragraph as one line, because GitHub and Slack render single newlines as visible breaks. This applies to PR/commit descriptions, Slack and Teams messages, issue comments, and any rendered markdown.
- Break lines only for structure: paragraphs, list items, headers, code blocks. Several distinct points need that structure, not one long paragraph.

## Writing style

- Never use em dashes (—) in prose. Use commas, colons, or periods.
- Human-facing text (plans, reports, user-facing messages): `asd-ste100` in STE-flavored mode, then `humanizer`.
- Text an agent or tool parses (tool and skill descriptions, error messages, system prompts, subagent prompts, inter-agent instructions, status reports): `asd-ste100`, strict mode for procedures, error messages, and tool descriptions.
- Not for creative or marketing copy.

## Personality and tone

Use all three in chat replies, each at its own layer:

- `caveman` (language): strip filler from the reply. Code is untouched.
- `ponytail` (code): take the laziest, most direct path. Prefer built-in features over new packages.
- `i-have-adhd` (structure): lead with the answer or action item. Brainstorm open-ended problems in independent parallel threads.

Scope: chat replies only. Persisted artifacts (commits, PRs, issues, docs, memory files, Slack and Teams messages) stay in normal prose. Don't carry caveman's compression into them.

## Terraform

- Never upgrade or install Terraform via brew. Set the version with [tenv](https://github.com/tofuutils/tenv) (`tenv terraform install <version>`) and run the tenv-managed binary, so it matches each project's `required_version`.
- For AWS resources, prefer registry modules from [terraform-aws-modules](https://github.com/terraform-aws-modules) and [aws-ia](https://github.com/aws-ia) over raw resources whenever a suitable module exists.
- Pick by task: `terraform-generator` writes new `.tf`/HCL, `terraform-validator` lints/audits it (tflint, checkov), `terraform` (MCP) handles plan previews, state inspection, module docs, and drift detection on existing code.
- Terragrunt: `terragrunt-skill` for architecture (stacks, units, catalog patterns, CI/CD, AWS/GCP OIDC, multi-account layouts); `terragrunt-generator`/`terragrunt-validator` for single-file HCL.

## Skill triggers

- After writing or modifying code, use `code-simplifier` before declaring the task done.
- AWS IAM, Secrets Manager, CloudFormation/CDK, containers, observability: `aws-core`.
- Up-to-date library/API docs instead of training-cutoff knowledge: `context7`.
- Writing or pressure-testing documentation against the code: `grill-with-docs`.
- Codebase structure, dependency, or "where does X connect" questions: run `/graphify` once, then `graphify query` before broad file searches.
- Debugging: `superpowers:systematic-debugging`. Before declaring done: `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Planning complex work: `superpowers:writing-plans`.
- ML-training work: `andrej-karpathy-skills`.
- Kubernetes manifests/Helm/Kustomize/policies: `kubernetes-skill` for security/reliability-sensitive work (insecure defaults, resource starvation, network exposure, privilege sprawl, fragile rollouts, API drift). `k8s-yaml-generator`/`k8s-yaml-validator` for quick, low-stakes scaffolding/linting.

## Elephant-Goldfish Model (`egm@egm`)

[Elephant-Goldfish Model](https://github.com/google/elephant-goldfish-model) drafts with project context (elephant) and reviews with a fresh agent that did not see the earlier chat (goldfish). You approve the design before implementation. It does not replace scanners (`trivy`, `tfsec`, `checkov`, `tflint`, `terrascan`).

Claude Code commands use the `/egm:` prefix. Cursor support is experimental: pick the skill from the slash menu, or read `skills/<name>/SKILL.md`. A goldfish review needs a fresh subagent. If the host cannot start one, stop. Do not simulate the review in the parent chat.

- New design or trade-off (IAM boundary, network policy, SLO, runbook, incident process, module split): `egm-brainstorm`, then `egm-draft`, then `egm-review`. Save the approved spec under `docs/designs/`.
- Existing design, runbook, or PR plan: `egm-review <path>`.
- Checked-in docs vs current Terraform, Helm, or manifests: `egm-drift-report <dir>`. Update the doc or the code. You choose which.
- Review findings: `egm-resolve`.
- Inherited service with no spec: `egm-bootstrap`, then review the generated docs.
- A named standard (change policy, a benchmark): `egm-review-loop`. This is not a Vanta control and not a sign-off.

## DevOps skills (`devops-skills@akin-ozer`)

Most domains have a `<domain>-generator` (create) and a `<domain>-validator` (lint/audit/fix). Domains: `ansible`, `azure-pipelines`, `bash-script`, `dockerfile`, `fluentbit`, `github-actions`, `gitlab-ci`, `helm`, `jenkinsfile`, `k8s-yaml`, `makefile`, `promql`, `terraform`, `terragrunt`.

- Generate-only: `logql-generator` (Loki LogQL), `loki-config-generator` (Loki server configs).
- `k8s-debug` diagnoses a *running* cluster via `kubectl`. For manifest files use `k8s-yaml-generator`/`k8s-yaml-validator`.

## Manual-install skills

Bare `SKILL.md` folders from `ai.manual_skills` (cloned into `~/.claude/skills/`, linked into `~/.cursor/skills/`). Routing notes:

- `eks-operation-review` audits a live EKS cluster ("how is the cluster"). `eks-upgrade-check` scores upgrade readiness ("about to upgrade").
- `k8s-debug` first for a broad look. Use `k8s-dns-troubleshooting`, `k8s-network-troubleshooting`, or `k8s-namespace-troubleshooting` when the problem is already DNS-, network-, or namespace-scoped, or `k8s-debug` didn't resolve it.
- `eks-resilience-checker` output feeds `chaos-engineering-on-aws`.
- `security-audit` is clone-only. It is not the official `security-guidance` plugin.
- `composio` routes Composio work. The `composio` plugin (Claude) and the `composio` MCP server (Cursor) supply the tools.

## Slack and Microsoft Teams messages

Rules for every Slack message and Teams message (chat, channel post, or reply).

- Always create it as a draft. Never send it. Sending is the user's call.
- Audience is developers and tech managers. Friendly tone, short direct text, no filler.
- Open with one line, then short blocks with a bold label (for example **Issue**, **Plan**, **Need from you**), a blank line between blocks. Use bold for the key point, a name, or a number.
- Numbered list for sequential steps, bullets for parallel items, one line per item.
- Run it through `humanizer` before drafting.
